---
// Publish Rails API documentation from rswag request specs: checked against
// the OpenAPI reference (route, code samples, Try it, CORS, authorization,
// spec warnings), navigation tabs, deployment, and validate docs, and on main
// (cf03b9cf, package 2.1.3) against the operation routes and their
// method-and-path fallback (openapi/model.ts operationKey, extractOperations),
// the spec parser and 3.0 upgrade (openapi/parse.ts), example resolution
// (components/openapi/helpers.ts declaredExample, operation-model.ts,
// RequestPanel.astro), and `blume init` (cli/commands/init.ts). rswag side:
// the rswag-specs 2.17.0 gem source (the swaggerize rake task, Configuration,
// SwaggerFormatter, RequestFactory, ResponseValidator, ExtendedSchema, the
// install generator and spec template) and the README at tag 2.17.0
// (802c6894537513a913a72e546d54b67f54305853), compared with the README on
// master, which documents the 3.0 API (on RubyGems only as the 3.0.0.pre
// prerelease, whose generator writes openapi_helper.rb and whose request
// factory reads `request_params`). Rails side: the
// actionpack 8.1.4 token regex, `load_defaults 8.1` params wrapping, and the
// generated Gemfile, cors.rb, Dockerfile, and .dockerignore.
//
// Ran end to end on 2026-10-05 with Ruby 4.0.7 (Homebrew), Rails 8.1.4,
// rspec-rails 8.0.4, rswag-specs 2.17.0, json-schema 6.2.1, rack-cors 3.0.0,
// sqlite3 2.9.6, Node 24.14.1, and Blume 2.1.3 symlinked from main and run by
// path in place of `npx blume` (gems bundled into vendor/bundle, because the
// Homebrew gem directory wasn't writable). Every command and file on this
// page, in a fresh folder, with the files on this page compared byte for
// byte with the final probe: `rails new`, the generators, `bundle exec
// rspec` (11 examples, 0 failures), `bin/rubocop` (no offenses), the
// swaggerize task with and without RSWAG_DRY_RUN=0 (the dry run wrote no
// examples; two real runs wrote identical bytes), `blume init --no-install`,
// `build`, `validate --strict` (exit 0), `audit` (0 errors, 0 warnings), and
// `preview` with curl: 200 on every route in the table, page text checked
// for titles, descriptions, Authorization sections, the Ruby tab, recorded
// request and response examples, and Try it's prefilled body. Try it in
// Chrome against `bin/rails server`, with the preview's origin added to the
// CORS list for the test: 401 without a key, 201 with one; curl preflights
// from the allowed and another origin. `blume dev` reloaded a page after the
// task rewrote the file. The workflow's run steps in a fresh clone (db
// prepare and swaggerize, the freshness check, validate, build); the
// archived_at scenario (3 failures under strict properties, then the
// freshness check failing with the diff shown and passing after the commit).
// Also probed: a failing spec (exit 1, file still written, its example left
// out, and recorded wrong without the exception guard); `rswag_dry_run =
// false` in config/environments/test.rb having no effect, with and without
// RAILS_ENV=test; `openapi: "3.1.0"` leaving `nullable` as written; routes
// without operationId; PATTERN writing only the matching paths; the errors
// for a missing `let(:Authorization)` and a missing `type: :request`; a
// broken link in an operation description failing validate; `rails g
// rswag:install` not existing without the `rswag` gem. Not run: `npm ci` and
// `npm install` (Blume was linked), the workflow on GitHub Actions (the
// setup, upload, and deploy actions), a Pages deploy, and PostgreSQL.
//
// Review, same day, in a copy of the probe: the swaggerize task again
// (identical bytes), `bundle exec rspec` and `bin/rubocop`, `build`,
// `validate --strict` (and a broken description link failing it), `audit`,
// the two NoMethodError messages, CORS preflights and the 401 with curl
// against `bin/rails server`, and `bin/rails runner` creating a key.
// Corrected from that: Blume doesn't publish the spec file (dist/openapi.json
// is Blume's own JSON API description), so rswag-api isn't replaced by it;
// the generated Dockerfile's BUNDLE_WITHOUT="development" still installs the
// :development, :test group in the image, so it's "doesn't load", not "ships
// neither"; and in a real run the helper's hook overwrites
// `request_body_example` entries (a dry run keeps them).
